1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,976
Senior Angular interview preparation, lesson 6.

2
00:00:02,976 --> 00:00:05,952
Testing Angular Libraries Through Consumer Behavior.

3
00:00:08,160 --> 00:00:09,687
Green tests need meaningful assertions

4
00:00:10,813 --> 00:00:12,340
Welcome to the testing lesson.

5
00:00:12,340 --> 00:00:15,394
The question shows tabs that shipped without keyboard support and

6
00:00:15,394 --> 00:00:18,143
with duplicate identifiers even though the tests were green.

7
00:00:18,143 --> 00:00:21,808
A passing test is evidence only for the assertion it actually makes.

8
00:00:21,808 --> 00:00:25,474
Checking that a select method exists proves the shape of an object.

9
00:00:25,474 --> 00:00:29,445
It does not prove a user can select a tab with the keyboard.

10
00:00:30,820 --> 00:00:33,231
Green tests need meaningful assertions · 2

11
00:00:34,356 --> 00:00:38,833
A test can even preserve a bug if it expects the wrong result.

12
00:00:38,833 --> 00:00:41,933
An assertion that the active class is absent after

13
00:00:41,933 --> 00:00:45,032
selection does not validate selection; it blesses that absence.

14
00:00:45,032 --> 00:00:48,132
Begin by writing the user-visible contract in plain language.

15
00:00:48,132 --> 00:00:50,887
A tab is selected, its panel is visible,

16
00:00:50,887 --> 00:00:53,298
focus moves according to the keyboard pattern,

17
00:00:53,298 --> 00:00:55,365
and identifiers remain unique across instances.

18
00:00:56,740 --> 00:00:59,400
Green tests need meaningful assertions · 3

19
00:01:00,525 --> 00:01:04,325
The testing strategy should reflect how the library is consumed.

20
00:01:04,325 --> 00:01:09,265
Product teams bind inputs in templates, project content, handle outputs, and connect forms.

21
00:01:09,265 --> 00:01:14,585
A component created alone with direct private method calls does not exercise those boundaries.

22
00:01:14,585 --> 00:01:18,005
Use a small host that resembles a real consumer,

23
00:01:18,005 --> 00:01:21,805
then interact through the rendered interface and assert observable results.

24
00:01:23,180 --> 00:01:25,126
A host exercises Angular integration

25
00:01:26,251 --> 00:01:30,143
A host template gives Angular the opportunity to bind inputs,

26
00:01:30,143 --> 00:01:34,035
create projected children, connect models, and resolve parent-provided injection contexts.

27
00:01:34,035 --> 00:01:38,317
This matters for tabs because the group discovers projected tab components.

28
00:01:38,317 --> 00:01:41,431
Calling a method on an isolated group cannot

29
00:01:41,431 --> 00:01:44,545
prove that discovery or its panel relationships work.

30
00:01:45,920 --> 00:01:48,394
A host exercises Angular integration · 2

31
00:01:49,519 --> 00:01:51,286
Render more than one instance.

32
00:01:51,286 --> 00:01:54,467
Duplicate identifiers are often invisible in a single fixture.

33
00:01:54,467 --> 00:01:57,648
A second group also catches selectors that accidentally operate

34
00:01:57,648 --> 00:02:00,829
on the entire document instead of the current component.

35
00:02:00,829 --> 00:02:04,363
Give the groups different names and labels so a test

36
00:02:04,363 --> 00:02:07,545
can locate the intended instance without relying on position.

37
00:02:08,919 --> 00:02:11,373
A host exercises Angular integration · 3

38
00:02:12,498 --> 00:02:14,950
Change consumer inputs after the initial render.

39
00:02:14,950 --> 00:02:17,752
A component may initialize correctly and then ignore

40
00:02:17,752 --> 00:02:20,554
a reset or a new set of children.

41
00:02:20,554 --> 00:02:24,758
Test removal, empty content, and valid dynamic changes if they are supported.

42
00:02:24,758 --> 00:02:29,312
This shifts the test from a constructor snapshot to the contract over time.

43
00:02:29,312 --> 00:02:32,464
A shared component lives inside an application that changes.

44
00:02:33,840 --> 00:02:35,993
Interactions should exercise the event path

45
00:02:37,118 --> 00:02:39,629
Directly calling select skips the event binding,

46
00:02:39,629 --> 00:02:41,781
key handling, default prevention, focus movement,

47
00:02:41,781 --> 00:02:42,858
and disabled checks.

48
00:02:42,858 --> 00:02:45,728
For a click contract, click the real control.

49
00:02:45,728 --> 00:02:48,957
For a keyboard contract, focus the relevant element and

50
00:02:48,957 --> 00:02:51,827
send the actual key in a browser test.

51
00:02:51,827 --> 00:02:53,979
Synthetic dispatch can exercise a handler,

52
00:02:53,979 --> 00:02:57,208
but it does not reproduce browser default actions such

53
00:02:57,208 --> 00:02:58,284
as Tab traversal.

54
00:02:59,660 --> 00:03:02,406
Interactions should exercise the event path · 2

55
00:03:03,532 --> 00:03:05,935
Write assertions from the chosen interaction table.

56
00:03:05,935 --> 00:03:10,399
In automatic tabs, ArrowRight moves focus and selection, wrapping at the final tab.

57
00:03:10,399 --> 00:03:12,460
Home and End reach the edges.

58
00:03:12,460 --> 00:03:15,207
Exactly one tab should be in the tab

59
00:03:15,207 --> 00:03:17,954
order, and its selected panel should be visible.

60
00:03:17,954 --> 00:03:20,014
Manual activation has a different table,

61
00:03:20,014 --> 00:03:22,761
so the test must not assume both patterns

62
00:03:22,761 --> 00:03:23,105
simultaneously.

63
00:03:24,480 --> 00:03:27,161
Interactions should exercise the event path · 3

64
00:03:28,286 --> 00:03:31,972
Use roles, accessible names, and supported harness methods to locate behavior.

65
00:03:31,972 --> 00:03:35,994
A private class used for styling is a fragile consumer test selector.

66
00:03:35,994 --> 00:03:39,681
The library may use internal selectors inside its harness because it

67
00:03:39,681 --> 00:03:43,367
owns both, but product tests should not need to know them.

68
00:03:43,367 --> 00:03:47,725
Test the result rather than the implementation detail that happened to create it.

69
00:03:49,100 --> 00:03:51,243
Harnesses are versioned testing interfaces

70
00:03:52,368 --> 00:03:57,082
A component harness exposes meaningful operations such as selectTab and getSelectedTabLabel.

71
00:03:57,082 --> 00:03:59,653
It encapsulates queries, interactions, and stabilization.

72
00:03:59,653 --> 00:04:03,939
A filter can choose a group by its public label.

73
00:04:03,939 --> 00:04:08,224
This creates a boundary between product tests and component internals.

74
00:04:09,600 --> 00:04:12,178
Harnesses are versioned testing interfaces · 2

75
00:04:13,303 --> 00:04:17,275
Expose the capabilities a consumer needs to verify a task.

76
00:04:17,275 --> 00:04:20,454
Do not return internal DOM nodes, private fields,

77
00:04:20,454 --> 00:04:24,427
or CSS class names merely to make the current test

78
00:04:24,427 --> 00:04:24,824
easy.

79
00:04:24,824 --> 00:04:26,811
Those leaks defeat the abstraction.

80
00:04:26,811 --> 00:04:30,386
Errors should explain when a requested tab does not

81
00:04:30,386 --> 00:04:33,565
exist rather than silently selecting an arbitrary fallback.

82
00:04:34,940 --> 00:04:37,258
Harnesses are versioned testing interfaces · 3

83
00:04:38,383 --> 00:04:42,281
Publish harnesses through a testing entry point and document supported environments.

84
00:04:42,281 --> 00:04:44,762
Keep the harness and component versions compatible.

85
00:04:44,762 --> 00:04:47,597
If a component's markup changes, the harness implementation

86
00:04:47,597 --> 00:04:50,433
can adapt while its task API stays stable.

87
00:04:50,433 --> 00:04:53,623
A harness API change itself can break consumer tests,

88
00:04:53,623 --> 00:04:57,876
so review and version it with the same care as an input

89
00:04:57,876 --> 00:04:58,584
or output.

90
00:04:59,960 --> 00:05:02,013
Stabilization must not hide rendering bugs

91
00:05:03,138 --> 00:05:05,875
Angular tests can force a render with detectChanges.

92
00:05:05,875 --> 00:05:10,667
That is useful for many assertions, but it can mask a missing change-detection notification.

93
00:05:10,667 --> 00:05:15,116
A promise callback that writes a plain field may appear correct when the

94
00:05:15,116 --> 00:05:19,565
test manually runs change detection even though the application would not render it.

95
00:05:20,940 --> 00:05:23,962
Stabilization must not hide rendering bugs · 2

96
00:05:25,087 --> 00:05:28,286
For notification-sensitive behavior, use a zoneless fixture and

97
00:05:28,286 --> 00:05:31,086
wait for stability without forcing every update.

98
00:05:31,086 --> 00:05:35,885
A signal consumed in the template, setInput, or markForCheck should notify Angular.

99
00:05:35,885 --> 00:05:39,085
Test an asynchronous completion and a timer reset

100
00:05:39,085 --> 00:05:41,885
through the same path the component uses.

101
00:05:41,885 --> 00:05:45,484
This verifies scheduling as well as the final value.

102
00:05:46,860 --> 00:05:49,589
Stabilization must not hide rendering bugs · 3

103
00:05:50,714 --> 00:05:53,890
Control time where the behavior depends on a deadline.

104
00:05:53,890 --> 00:05:58,478
Fake timers or an injected scheduler can make a debounce or timeout deterministic.

105
00:05:58,478 --> 00:06:01,302
But do not replace the event or scheduling

106
00:06:01,302 --> 00:06:04,126
mechanism that is the subject of the test.

107
00:06:04,126 --> 00:06:07,655
Keep at least one browser integration case for layout, focus,

108
00:06:07,655 --> 00:06:11,185
and native behavior that an emulated DOM cannot represent accurately.

109
00:06:12,560 --> 00:06:14,479
Accessibility automation and its limits

110
00:06:15,604 --> 00:06:18,290
An accessibility scanner can detect missing names,

111
00:06:18,290 --> 00:06:21,745
invalid ARIA relationships, and many contrast failures in a

112
00:06:21,745 --> 00:06:22,513
rendered browser.

113
00:06:22,513 --> 00:06:25,967
Run it on relevant component states: initial, selected, invalid,

114
00:06:25,967 --> 00:06:29,422
expanded, and disabled where those states change the tree.

115
00:06:29,422 --> 00:06:33,645
A single empty fixture tells you little about a composite control.

116
00:06:35,020 --> 00:06:37,409
Accessibility automation and its limits · 2

117
00:06:38,535 --> 00:06:41,003
Keyboard and focus need explicit behavior tests.

118
00:06:41,003 --> 00:06:44,882
A scanner can accept a tablist whose ArrowRight handler does nothing.

119
00:06:44,882 --> 00:06:48,408
Live regions need a different kind of evidence: verify that

120
00:06:48,408 --> 00:06:51,934
a persistent connected region receives the intended message with the

121
00:06:51,934 --> 00:06:55,108
chosen politeness, then manually check supported screen reader combinations.

122
00:06:55,108 --> 00:07:00,045
Do not claim a DOM assertion proves that a user heard the exact sentence.

123
00:07:01,420 --> 00:07:04,447
Accessibility automation and its limits · 3

124
00:07:05,573 --> 00:07:10,762
Combine tests rather than letting one category stand in for all others.

125
00:07:10,762 --> 00:07:13,357
Compiler checks prove types and templates.

126
00:07:13,357 --> 00:07:15,952
Browser tests prove the exercised behavior.

127
00:07:15,952 --> 00:07:18,547
Visual checks prove the reviewed appearance.

128
00:07:18,547 --> 00:07:23,737
Manual assistive-technology testing covers interaction and announcements that automation cannot fully certify.

129
00:07:23,737 --> 00:07:26,765
Record those boundaries in the release evidence.

130
00:07:28,140 --> 00:07:30,118
Make visual regression coverage intentional

131
00:07:31,243 --> 00:07:34,804
A large screenshot suite can be noisy when every

132
00:07:34,804 --> 00:07:38,365
story is captured in every theme at every viewport.

133
00:07:38,365 --> 00:07:41,531
Start by mapping the behaviors each image protects.

134
00:07:41,531 --> 00:07:45,092
Keep representative states, themes, density variants, and text-growth cases.

135
00:07:45,092 --> 00:07:49,445
Remove redundant captures only after confirming their risk is covered elsewhere.

136
00:07:50,820 --> 00:07:53,604
Make visual regression coverage intentional · 2

137
00:07:54,729 --> 00:07:59,103
Control the environment: fonts, viewport, locale, animation, loading data, and time.

138
00:07:59,103 --> 00:08:03,876
Wait for a meaningful ready state rather than an arbitrary long sleep.

139
00:08:03,876 --> 00:08:05,466
Diagnose flakes by category.

140
00:08:05,466 --> 00:08:09,444
A random data value is different from a late web

141
00:08:09,444 --> 00:08:13,421
font, and a layout race is different from antialiasing noise.

142
00:08:13,421 --> 00:08:16,205
Fix the cause before loosening comparison thresholds.

143
00:08:17,580 --> 00:08:20,223
Make visual regression coverage intentional · 3

144
00:08:21,348 --> 00:08:23,236
Visual tests need reviewable baselines.

145
00:08:23,236 --> 00:08:27,767
A baseline update should identify the intended change and the affected states.

146
00:08:27,767 --> 00:08:32,299
Do not approve thousands of images blindly or turn off failures globally.

147
00:08:32,299 --> 00:08:36,831
Use focused diffs, ownership, and deterministic fixtures so the suite remains a

148
00:08:36,831 --> 00:08:40,985
useful signal rather than a recurring cost everyone learns to ignore.

149
00:08:42,360 --> 00:08:44,467
Prove the regression test detects the defect

150
00:08:45,592 --> 00:08:48,300
When fixing a bug, make sure the new test

151
00:08:48,300 --> 00:08:51,008
fails against the old behavior for the intended reason.

152
00:08:51,008 --> 00:08:53,416
If the original tabs lack a keyboard handler,

153
00:08:53,416 --> 00:08:56,425
an ArrowRight test should fail because focus and selection do

154
00:08:56,425 --> 00:08:59,434
not change, not because the fixture has an unrelated missing

155
00:08:59,434 --> 00:08:59,735
import.

156
00:08:59,735 --> 00:09:02,745
This is the evidence that the test protects the correction.

157
00:09:04,120 --> 00:09:07,368
Prove the regression test detects the defect · 2

158
00:09:08,493 --> 00:09:11,019
Avoid assertions that merely mirror the source.

159
00:09:11,019 --> 00:09:14,268
Checking that a function assigns a field is weaker

160
00:09:14,268 --> 00:09:17,155
than observing the resulting selected panel and focus.

161
00:09:17,155 --> 00:09:20,765
Test the public result with realistic input and multiple instances.

162
00:09:20,765 --> 00:09:24,735
Where the contract has boundary cases, include the smallest cases that

163
00:09:24,735 --> 00:09:28,345
expose them rather than a huge collection of near-duplicate examples.

164
00:09:29,720 --> 00:09:32,592
Prove the regression test detects the defect · 3

165
00:09:33,717 --> 00:09:36,361
In the upcoming question, we will replace method-existence

166
00:09:36,361 --> 00:09:39,005
checks with host-based interactions and a supported harness.

167
00:09:39,005 --> 00:09:41,318
We will explain what the harness hides,

168
00:09:41,318 --> 00:09:43,631
what remains part of its public API,

169
00:09:43,631 --> 00:09:46,276
and how accessibility and visual checks complement it.

170
00:09:46,276 --> 00:09:49,250
The goal is a test suite that tells the

171
00:09:49,250 --> 00:09:52,225
library team whether consumers can still perform their tasks.

172
00:09:53,600 --> 00:09:55,364
TST-001 · Scenario

173
00:09:56,489 --> 00:10:01,077
We now review TST-001: Tests that pass while the component is broken.

174
00:10:01,077 --> 00:10:05,665
Inspect the consumer contract and identify the mechanism behind each reported defect.

